<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ulrich, Duke of Württemberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1487">1487</a>–<a href="/years/1550">1550</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ulrich, Duke of Württemberg, ruled from 1498 to 1550. His heavy taxes provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poor-conrad">Poor Conrad</a> revolt of 1514, and after he seized the imperial city of Reutlingen in 1519 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-league">Swabian League</a> drove him out, leaving the duchy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule. Restored in 1534 with the help of Philip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, he introduced the Lutheran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ulrich-duke-of-wurttemberg.jpg" alt="Ulrich, Duke of Württemberg" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:900-121_Herzog_Ulrich.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ulrich was born on 8 February 1487, and his mother died at his birth. His father, Henry, Count of Württemberg, was mentally deranged, probably after three years of imprisonment by Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, and was confined to Hohenurach Castle. Ulrich succeeded his kinsman Eberhard II in 1498 and was declared of age in 1503. He served King Maximilian I in the War of the Succession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a> in 1504, gaining some lands for Württemberg, and joined the imperial army that besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a> in 1513.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His extravagance ran up large debts, and his oppressive ways of raising money made him unpopular. He married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabina">Sabina</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-iv-duke-of-bavaria">Albert IV, Duke of Bavaria</a>, and niece of Maximilian, in 1511, but the marriage was unhappy, and in 1515 he killed the knight Hans von Hutten, whose wife he admired. Sabina fled to the emperor and her brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iv-duke-of-bavaria">William IV, Duke of Bavaria</a>, and Ulrich was twice placed under the imperial ban. Driven out in 1519, he spent years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, announced his conversion to the reformed faith about 1523, and failed in an invasion of February 1525 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-peasants-war">German Peasants' War</a>, signing himself Ulrich the peasant.</p><p>His friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-i-landgrave-of-hesse">Philip I, Landgrave of Hesse</a>, invaded Württemberg in April 1534 and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> troops at the Battle of Lauffen on 13 May. By the treaty negotiated at Kaaden in June 1534, which Ulrich signed in February 1535, King Ferdinand I recognised him as duke under Austrian suzerainty. Many convents and monasteries were destroyed, and the seizure of church property helped his empty treasury.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Heavy taxes soon cost him his popularity again. He joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a> in April 1536, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-war">Schmalkaldic War</a> of 1546 the duchy was quickly overrun. By the Treaty of Heilbronn of January 1547 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> let him keep it, but he had to pay a large sum, give up fortresses and appear as a suppliant before the emperor at Ulm, and in May 1548 he submitted to the Augsburg Interim. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a> on 6 November 1550 and was succeeded by his son Christopher. His volatile character led historians to call him the Swabian Henry VIII.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ulrich, Duke of Württemberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successor <b>Ulrich</b> proved a violent ruler, and the peasant revolt of the Poor Conrad in 1514 forced him to accept the Treaty of Tübingen, guaranteeing the estates' rights.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-wurttemberg">Duchy of Württemberg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Riquewihr, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.17,7.3&amp;year=1527" aria-label="Riquewihr on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ulrich, Duke of Württemberg"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5415 5982h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5415 5982h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.17,7.3&amp;year=1527">What was here in 1527? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
